---
import BlogLayout from "../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import { posts } from "../../data/blog";
import { profile } from "../../data/profile";
const post = posts.find(
  (p) => p.slug === "apple-design-award-finalist-wwdc26",
)!;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BlogLayout
  title={`${post.title}, Lilly Seay`}
  description={post.description}
  image={post.cover}
>
  <article class="post prose-post">
    <header class="post-head wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Blog · Apple Design Awards 2026 and WWDC26</p>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p class="post-meta">
        By {profile.name} · {post.date} · {post.readTime}
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap post-body">
      <figure class="li-embed">
        <iframe
          src="https://www.linkedin.com/embed/feed/update/urn:li:ugcPost:7471272537973252096?compact=1"
          height="399"
          width="504"
          frameborder="0"
          allowfullscreen=""
          title="WWDC26 vlog as an Apple Design Award Finalist, embedded from LinkedIn"
        ></iframe>
        <figcaption>
          My WWDC26 vlog as an Apple Design Award Finalist.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        For the last decade of designing apps, I've had one dream: an Apple
        Design Award. This May, Apple named Hearing Buddy a 2026 Apple Design
        Award Finalist in Inclusivity. What follows is the story of that week,
        from the email to the Observatory at Apple Park.
      </p>
      <h2>The announcement</h2>
      <p>
        What's most special to me is that Hearing Buddy is a Finalist in
        Inclusivity. As a disabled female founder, inclusivity and accessibility
        are the backbone of everything I do. The Hearing Buddy brand is a
        colorful expression of my disability pride and my commitment to making
        hearing solutions accessible to all.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/top-linkedin-posts/02-ada-finalist.jpg`}
          alt="Apple Design Awards 2026 Finalist graphic for Hearing Buddy"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Apple Design Awards 2026 Finalist, Inclusivity.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        It was bittersweet, too. I went through my hearing loss journey with my
        real-life hearing buddy, Scott Krager. We built the app together, and
        now we were Apple Design Award Finalists together. He's my biggest
        supporter in every part of my life. The Finalist build was my best
        Liquid Glass work yet. I put my heart and soul into the UI and UX. And
        none of it would have happened without my partnership manager,
        Christopher Tan, and everyone at Apple who rooted for Hearing Buddy.
      </p>
      <p>
        The winners were announced before the conference. Guitar Wiz took the
        Inclusivity award, and it earned it. I sent my congratulations and
        started packing. We were going to WWDC26.
      </p>
      <h2>Getting to Cupertino</h2>
      <p>
        Officially in Cupertino as an ADA Finalist, I did the only reasonable
        thing and took a Waymo. The next morning I was up early to pick up my
        badge and see the WWDC26 swag. Then it was time.
      </p>
      <h2>WWDC26</h2>
      <p>
        We were at WWDC26. The day before the keynote, Apple hosted a reception
        for all of the Apple Design Award Finalists and winners at the badge
        event. So much excellence in one photo. We got to showcase Hearing Buddy
        and talk to a bunch of people, and I loved sharing how Apple's latest
        technologies have changed my life in the most positive way by helping me
        hear better in conversations.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/finalists-group.jpg`}
          alt="The 2026 Apple Design Award Finalists and winners gathered around the WWDC26 sign at Apple Park"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          All of the Apple Design Award Finalists and winners at the WWDC26
          badge event.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Of course, I took a picture with the ADA award, like I do every time I
        go to WWDC. It's my dream to win one. Apple thought through every detail
        to make the ADA experience special, and I had so much fun that day with
        Scott.
      </p>
      <figure class="video-figure small">
        <video
          controls
          playsinline
          preload="metadata"
          poster={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/inclusivity-display-poster.jpg`}
        >
          <source
            src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/inclusivity-display.mp4`}
            type="video/mp4"
          />
        </video>
        <figcaption>
          Hearing Buddy on the Inclusivity Finalists display at WWDC26.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/caption-with-pride.jpg`}
          alt="Lilly with the Inclusivity winner and Scott, all wearing caption with pride shirts"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          With the Inclusivity winner. We were Finalists in the same category.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2>The State of the Union</h2>
      <p>
        Then the keynote. I still can't believe the Hearing Buddy logo was in
        the State of the Union. A few things I really loved: Apple Intelligence,
        SiriAI, and CoreAI, because we are following Apple's lead in the AI
        space and cannot wait to use these in Hearing Buddy. The child safety
        features, which would help us make a version of Hearing Buddy for kids.
        And the Liquid Glass improvements. We already have a Hearing Buddy build
        running on iOS 27, and it looked better with no coding needed.
      </p>
      <p>
        I was impressed at Apple's dedication to taking feedback from developers
        and making real improvements. We can't wait to start coding iOS 27
        features and dive even deeper into our Liquid Glass designs.
      </p>
      <h2>The Observatory</h2>
      <p>
        The peak of the week was demoing Hearing Buddy in the Observatory at
        Apple Park. Walking in for the first time, it was the most beautiful
        architecture I've ever experienced.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/hello-selfie.jpg`}
          alt="Lilly and Scott in front of the hello sculpture inside the Observatory at Apple Park"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Seeing the Observatory for the first time.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Inside, we demoed Hearing Buddy to Apple and to fellow Finalists. We
        also met the founding team of Tiimo, who won App of the Year in 2025. It
        was the best week ever.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure two">
        <div>
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/showing-hearing-buddy.jpg`}
            alt="Lilly holding up Hearing Buddy on her iPhone in the Observatory"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/developer-center-table.jpg`}
            alt="Lilly at a table with her phone between demos"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Demoing Hearing Buddy in the Observatory.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2>What it meant</h2>
      <p>
        I built Hearing Buddy because I needed it. Being recognized in the
        Inclusivity category, specifically, is the version of this award I would
        have picked. Being there as a Finalist, with Scott, showing the app to
        the people who make the platform, is a week I'll carry for a long time.
        We'll be back.
      </p>

      <section class="post-outro reveal">
        <h2>Apple's 2026 Apple Design Awards</h2>
        <p>
          Hearing Buddy is listed as a Finalist in the Inclusivity category on
          <a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/awards/"
            >Apple's Design Awards page</a
          >, in
          <a
            href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-reveals-winners-of-the-2026-apple-design-awards/"
            >Apple's newsroom announcement of the 2026 winners</a
          >, and in the App Store story
          <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/iphone/story/id1896567319"
            >Meet the 2026 Apple Design Award Finalists</a
          >. Apple's description: "Created by a solo developer with hearing
          loss, Hearing Buddy is shaped by experience. The thoughtful app uses
          the Foundation Models framework and on-device speech-to-text features
          to quickly generate real-time captions and summaries of spoken
          conversations."
        </p>
        <p>
          The posts this story comes from are on LinkedIn:
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7462194542281142272/"
            >the announcement</a
          >,
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7469837105968435201/"
            >the badge event</a
          >,
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7470243318669447168/"
            >the State of the Union</a
          >, and
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7470542551654195200/"
            >the Observatory</a
          >. Building something for the App Store and want it to stand out? <a
            href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>Send me a note</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BlogLayout>
